## Project layout

```text
src/{{ package_name }}/
  application/
    models.py          # Cross-cutting domain models: scope registry, task queues
    schemas.py         # Cross-cutting request/response DTOs
    services.py        # Cross-cutting application services
    dependencies.py    # Shared dependency injection helpers (Annotated Dep aliases)
{% if include_example_endpoint %}
    items/               # Everything item-domain-specific
      models.py            # Item
      schemas.py            # ItemDTO, PaginatedItemsDTO
      services.py            # ItemService
      dependencies.py         # get_item_service, ItemServiceDep
{% endif %}
  core/
    config.py          # Configuration
    logging.py          # Structured logging
{% if enable_telemetry %}
    telemetry.py        # OpenTelemetry tracing
{% endif %}
{% if enable_azure_auth %}
    auth.py              # azure_scheme JWT validation + require_role()
{% endif %}
  database/
    session.py          # SQLAlchemy session management and ORM models
    queues.py            # Queue backend adapters
{% if include_example_endpoint %}
    repositories.py      # Repository implementations
{% endif %}
  api/
    router.py           # Top-level composition: system{% if enable_azure_auth %}, auth{% endif %} and tasks routers{% if include_example_endpoint %}, plus items_router{% endif %}, wired into one `router`
{% if include_example_endpoint %}
    items/
      router.py            # items_router (/items)
{% endif %}
  main.py                # Application entrypoint
  container.py            # ServiceContainer shape (no service imports at runtime)
  bootstrap.py            # Builds a ServiceContainer from Settings
tests/{{ package_name }}/
  application/
    test_models.py     # Domain model tests
    test_services.py   # Service tests
  api/
    test_routes.py     # Route integration tests
{% if enable_azure_auth %}
    test_auth.py         # /auth/me + /auth/admin tests via dependency overrides
{% endif %}
{% if include_example_endpoint %}
    test_items.py       # Example /items endpoint tests
{% endif %}
  database/
{% if include_example_endpoint %}
    test_repositories.py  # Repository tests against a real Postgres database
{% endif %}
    test_queues.py         # Queue adapter tests against a real Redis instance
```

`container.py` only defines the `ServiceContainer` dataclass (its field types
are `TYPE_CHECKING`-only imports); `bootstrap.py` does the actual wiring and
is the only module that imports the concrete services. Request-time code
(`application/dependencies.py`) imports `container.py`, not `bootstrap.py`, so it
never pulls in the full service graph just to get the container's shape.

{% if include_example_endpoint %}
### Adding a new domain

`items` is the template for grouping a growing domain's code together:
production code for a domain gets its own `application/<domain>/` folder
(models, schemas, services, dependencies) and its own `api/<domain>/router.py`,
while `api/router.py` just imports each domain's router and composes them
into one `router`. Cross-cutting things that don't belong to a single
domain — `HealthService`, `TaskQueue`, `ScopeRegistry` — stay in the shared
`application/` files instead of being forced into a domain folder. Not
every route needs this treatment: a single-endpoint router (like `/health`
or `/tasks/dispatch`) is fine defined directly in `api/router.py`; reach for
a domain folder once there's enough model/schema/service/route code to be
worth grouping.
{% endif %}

## Quick start

```bash
cp .env.example .env
uv sync
uv run {{ package_slug }}
```

The default `.env.example` points at the Postgres and Redis services defined in
`docker-compose.yml`. Start them first if you don't have your own instances running:

```bash
docker compose up -d db redis
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run {{ package_slug }}
```

`uv run {{ package_slug }}` is the canonical way to run the app locally; it
reads `host`/`port` from `Settings` (`.env`). Because the project depends on
`fastapi[standard]`, `uv run fastapi dev` and `uv run fastapi run` also work
(reading the `[tool.fastapi]` entrypoint in `pyproject.toml` instead of
`Settings`) if you want `fastapi dev`'s autoreload during development —
just note its `--host`/`--port` flags are independent of `{{ env_prefix }}HOST`/
`{{ env_prefix }}PORT`.

A `Makefile` wraps the common commands (`make install`, `make dev`, `make test`,
`make lint`, `make format`, `make typecheck`, `make check`, `make migrate`,
`make up`, `make hooks`) if you'd rather not type the `uv run ...` forms.

## Running with Docker

```bash
cp .env.example .env
docker compose up --build
```

This builds the application image and starts it alongside Postgres and Redis. The API
is available at `http://localhost:8000`. The image runs as a non-root user and
declares a `HEALTHCHECK` against `/health`.

## Quality checks

```bash
uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format .
uv run pyright
uv run pytest
uv run library-skills --tool-skill install --claude --yes --all
uv tool install prek
prek install -t pre-commit -t commit-msg -t pre-push
prek run --all-files
```

`prek install` with no `-t` flags only wires up the `pre-commit` stage; the
conventional-commit check (`commit-msg`) and the pytest gate (`pre-push`)
configured in `prek.toml` need their hook types installed explicitly.

## Migrations

```bash
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add new model"
```

## Testing

Tests run against a real Postgres database and a real Redis instance rather
than mocks, so both need to be reachable before running `uv run pytest`:

```bash
docker compose up -d db redis
```

By default tests use:

- `{{ env_prefix }}TEST_DATABASE_URL` (defaults to
  `postgresql+asyncpg://{{ package_name }}:{{ package_name }}@localhost:5432/{{ package_name }}_test`)
- `{{ env_prefix }}TEST_REDIS_URL` (defaults to `redis://localhost:6379/1`)

Override either if your local Postgres/Redis use different credentials or
ports. A session-scoped fixture in `tests/conftest.py` applies Alembic
migrations to the test database automatically before the suite runs. CI
provisions matching Postgres and Redis service containers (see
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`).

## Queue backends

Use environment variables to switch backends:

- `{{ env_prefix }}QUEUE_BACKEND=memory`
- `{{ env_prefix }}QUEUE_BACKEND=redis`
- `{{ env_prefix }}QUEUE_BACKEND=azure_storage_queue`

`azure-storage-queue` is an optional dependency (the `azure` extra), not a
default install, since most projects only need `memory` or `redis`. Install
it before switching to the Azure backend:

```bash
uv sync --extra azure
```

Selecting `azure_storage_queue` without the extra installed raises a clear
`ImportError` at queue-construction time telling you to run the command
above. To add a different backend (SQS, GCP Pub/Sub, ...), implement the
same `enqueue`/`ping`/`close` protocol as `RedisTaskQueue` in
`database/queues.py`, add it as its own optional extra in `pyproject.toml`,
and branch on it in `create_task_queue`.

Define scopes with JSON in `{{ env_prefix }}SCOPES`, for example:

```json
[
  {"name": "identity", "description": "Authentication and authorization."},
  {"name": "orders", "description": "Ordering workflows."}
]
```

## API conventions

- `/health` reports queue reachability and a real database liveness check
  (a pooled connection checkout through the async engine), not just URL-shape
  validation.
{% if enable_azure_auth -%}
- `/auth/me` and `/auth/admin` require a valid Entra ID bearer token (the
  latter also requires the `Admin` app role) — see
  [Authentication](#authentication-azure-entra-id).
{% endif -%}
{% if include_example_endpoint -%}
- `/items` is a real, Postgres-backed example endpoint demonstrating paginated
  collections (`page_size=20` by default, `100` as the maximum) with a
  request-scoped `AsyncSession` dependency and a repository. Replace it with
  your own domain endpoints.
{% endif -%}
- `/tasks/dispatch` queues long-running work to the configured queue backend.

Routes are grouped into per-domain `APIRouter`s in `api/router.py`
(`system_router`{% if enable_azure_auth %}, `auth_router`{% endif %}{% if include_example_endpoint %}, `items_router`{% endif %}, `tasks_router`), each declaring its own
`prefix`/`tags` rather than repeating them per route — follow the same
pattern when adding new domains.

## Correlation IDs and request tracing

All requests automatically receive a correlation ID for backend tracing:

- Correlation IDs are generated internally for every request (UUID v4).
- Correlation IDs are included in all response headers as `x-correlation-id`.
- Correlation IDs are propagated through logs in the format:
  ```
  [correlation_id=<id>{% if enable_telemetry %} trace_id=<trace> span_id=<span>{% endif %}]
  ```

Use the response header value to trace a request through backend logs:

```bash
curl -i http://localhost:8000/health
```

**Backend observability**: Correlation IDs are intended for server-side request
inspection. Clients do not supply them; downstream services should use the internally
generated value when propagating backend trace context.

{% if enable_azure_auth -%}
## Authentication (Azure Entra ID)

This service is a resource server, not an identity provider: it validates
bearer JWTs issued by Entra ID, it doesn't run a login page. Clients (a
frontend, another service, a script using client-credentials) authenticate
against Entra ID themselves and send the resulting access token as
`Authorization: Bearer <token>`.

### Required setup (single-tenant)

1. In Entra ID, create an app registration for this API (or reuse an
   existing one). Note its **Application (client) ID** and your
   **Directory (tenant) ID**.
2. Under "Expose an API", add a scope named `user_impersonation` (the
   default `azure_scheme` configuration expects `api://<client id>/user_impersonation`).
3. Under "App roles", add any roles you want to gate routes on (the example
   route below expects one named `Admin`), then assign them to users/groups/
   service principals in Enterprise Applications.
4. Set `{{ env_prefix }}AZURE_TENANT_ID` and `{{ env_prefix }}AZURE_CLIENT_ID` in `.env` — the
   app raises a clear `pydantic.ValidationError` at startup if either is
   missing.
5. If you also want Swagger UI's "Authorize" button to work, register a
   separate public client (SPA) app registration for it and set
   `{{ env_prefix }}AZURE_OPENAPI_CLIENT_ID`; otherwise it falls back to
   `{{ env_prefix }}AZURE_CLIENT_ID`.

### Using it

- `CurrentUserDep` (in `application/dependencies.py`) resolves to a validated
  `fastapi_azure_auth.user.User` for any endpoint requiring just "a logged-in
  caller" — see `GET /auth/me`.
- `require_role("SomeRole")` (in `core/auth.py`) builds a dependency that
  also 403s callers missing that Entra ID app role. `require_admin_role` is
  a ready-made example (`GET /auth/admin`); add more the same way for your
  own roles. `require_role` is just `get_current_user` wired to
  `check_role(role, user)`, a provider-agnostic role check that only
  assumes `user.roles` — swapping in another JWT-based provider (e.g. AWS
  Cognito, Sign in with Apple) later means writing a new identity-resolution
  dependency to replace `get_current_user`; `check_role` keeps working as-is.
- `azure_scheme` validates the token's signature (against Entra ID's
  published signing keys, fetched and cached automatically), issuer,
  audience, and expiry on every call. It's built per-container from that
  container's own settings (`core/auth.build_azure_scheme`), not a
  module-level singleton, so `create_app(settings=...)` overrides actually
  take effect.
- Auth is opt-in per router, not global: only routers that declare
  `dependencies=[Depends(get_current_user)]` (like the example `/items` and
  `/tasks` routers here) actually require a valid caller. A new router you
  add needs the same `dependencies=[...]` — enabling `enable_azure_auth`
  alone doesn't protect it.

### Testing

`tests/{{ package_name }}/api/test_auth.py` overrides `get_current_user` via
`app.dependency_overrides` with a fake `User` rather than validating real
tokens — `azure_scheme` needs live network access to Entra ID's signing
keys, which a unit test suite shouldn't depend on. Follow the same pattern
for your own protected routes; validate against a real tenant in a manual
or integration pass instead.

{% endif -%}
## Releasing

`.github/workflows/release.yml` runs Commitizen on every push to `main`: it
inspects [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) since
the last tag, bumps the version in `pyproject.toml`, updates `CHANGELOG.md`,
and pushes a `vX.Y.Z` tag. Nothing to do manually beyond following the commit
message convention (already enforced by the `commit-msg` hook).

## Agent skill discovery

Run `uv run library-skills --tool-skill install --claude --yes --all` after
dependency changes to refresh `.agents/skills` and `.claude/skills` symlinks
for libraries that ship agent skills.
